    "answer": "Reported in Credit Suisse Interview SF office The number of shares should be an average over the period (weighted average shares outstanding). EPS is calculated using Net Income (which accumulates over a period) divided by weighted average shares outstanding during that period. This matches the timing of earnings generation with share count. Using a point-in-time share count would misstate EPS if the company issued or repurchased shares during the period. The P/E ratio's numerator (current share price) is point-in-time, but the denominator (EPS) uses period-average shares for consistency with income statement reporting.",
