    "question": "Walk me through the accounting for a convertible bond at the time of issuance and upon conversion into common stock.",
    "answer": "Reported in J.P. Morgan Interview Chicago office At Issuance Under current ASC 470-20 (as amended by ASU 2020-06), most convertible debt is NOT bifurcated: Record the entire proceeds as a debt liability (at par plus/minus any discount or premium); amortize using the effective interest method. No separate equity component for the embedded conversion feature unless there is a distinct equity element (e.g., substantial premium or detachable equity instrument) allocated to APIC. BS: Debt (liability) and Cash (asset); APIC only if a separate equity component exists. Upon Conversion When bondholders convert under the original contractual terms: Remove the carrying value of the debt (including any unamortized discount/issuance costs) from the balance sheet. Issue common stock at par value. Any difference between the debt's carrying amount and common stock at par is credited to APIC (no gain or loss recognized). BS: Debt decreases; Common Stock and APIC together increase by the total carrying value reclassified to equity. Note: Simplified answer assumes a plain-vanilla convertible (no derivative bifurcation) and book-value conversion accounting.",
