    "question": "What are Net Operating Losses (NOLs) and how can a company use them?",
    "answer": "Reported in Moelis & Co. Interview A Net Operating Loss (NOL) occurs when a company’s expenses and deductions exceed its revenues in a period. That loss generates an NOL carryforward that can be applied against taxable income in future periods to lower cash tax payments. On the balance sheet, unused NOLs are recorded as deferred tax assets. The tax benefit of an NOL is only realized when it actually reduces cash taxes, often years after the loss is incurred. Because the value of that future benefit is discounted to present value, companies aim to use NOLs as soon as they have sufficient taxable income to maximize their benefit.",
    "topic": "Accounting",
