    "answer": "Reported in Moelis & Co. Interview In an inflationary environment, LIFO yields a higher COGS charge—reducing taxable income and cash taxes paid—so operating cash flow increases; FIFO has the opposite effect. How it works: LIFO assigns the most recent (higher-cost) inventory to COGS, lowering gross profit and taxable income, which reduces cash tax payments and boosts cash flow. FIFO uses older (lower-cost) inventory first, resulting in lower COGS, higher taxable income, increased cash tax payments, and thus lower operating cash flow.",
